 ## Background
-Feature Fields were introduced as a generalization of the NeRF formulation, allowing the prediction of arbitrary per-point feature signals rather than only color [S1, unofficial]. Semantic-NeRF was notably the first to introduce semantic fields within the NeRF framework, using ground-truth semantic segmentation maps to augment the radiance field with per-point semantic logits, enabling dense 3D semantic understanding [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent works have expanded this concept to include object codes [S2, unofficial], self-supervised segmentation features [S3], and geometric priors for 3D detection [S4].
+Feature Fields were introduced as a generalization of the NeRF formulation, allowing the network to predict arbitrary per-point feature signals $\mathbf{f}$ in addition to color $\mathbf{c}$ and density $\sigma$ [S1, unofficial]. This concept was first explicitly integrated into the NeRF framework by Semantic-NeRF, which used ground-truth semantic segmentation maps to augment the radiance field with per-point semantic logits, enabling dense 3D semantic understanding [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages the dual 2D–3D nature of NeRFs and the expressive capacity of pre-trained 2D foundation models to enable embodied agents to operate with spatially grounded semantics [S1, unofficial].
+
+Training and exploiting feature fields introduces notable challenges. The high dimensionality of feature embeddings substantially increases memory usage and computational cost [S1, unofficial]. Moreover, the process of lifting 2D features into 3D remains fundamentally 2D-driven and lacks the intrinsic geometric reasoning that explicit 3D encoders could provide [S1, unofficial]. Finally, unlike direct NeRF interpretation methods that infer semantics in a single forward pass, feature fields require per-scene optimization before their semantics can be exploited, limiting scalability for large datasets or real-time use [S1, unofficial]. Despite these limitations, feature fields constitute a powerful middle ground between reconstruction and interpretation, transforming NeRFs into a unified representation where meaning and structure coexist [S1, unofficial].
 
